WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.280
Instruction hierarchy is a way to express which instructions have higher authority.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.980
System and developer instructions are trusted configuration controlled by the application owner.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.800
User goals describe what the user wants within allowed boundaries.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:19.340
Retrieved evidence, uploaded files, tool outputs, and other external content should be treated as data.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:27.880
This hierarchy helps the model behave sensibly, but it is also a design reminder for developers: do not let lower-trust content become policy.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:32.100
Context design is the process of assembling the model input deliberately.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:42.560
A secure Python application should separate instructions from content, label sources, preserve provenance metadata, and keep each piece of context scoped to the current task.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:51.420
The application should avoid invisible authority in retrieved text, such as placing untrusted document text next to trusted policy without clear separation.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:56.040
The model and the user both benefit when the context explains what came from where.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:58.580
Context minimization is part of defense.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.680
More context is not always safer or more accurate.

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:12.460
Extra documents, stale history, unrelated tool outputs, and broad memory can increase exposure and create more opportunities for irrelevant or misleading text to influence behavior.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:19.640
The application should include only the information needed for the approved task, filtered by the user's permissions and the purpose of the workflow.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:25.060
Clear prompting can reduce confusion, but it is not a reliable security boundary by itself.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:37.200
A system instruction may say that untrusted content cannot change rules, but the Python application still needs to enforce authorization, retrieval access, tool permission, and approval requirements.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:41.260
Security-critical rules should be represented in code and policy checks.

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:44.760
Prompt wording should support those controls, not replace them.
